# Line endings: normalised to LF in the index and in the working tree.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, and with core.autocrlf=false (this repo's
# setting), git stores exactly the bytes the working tree holds. Tooling on
# Windows writes CRLF, so a three-line edit could land as a whole-file diff, and
# every contributor -- human or agent -- had to remember to strip carriage
# returns by hand after every write. That manual step was forgotten repeatedly:
# 16 files reached main with CRLF before this file landed, and one ended up
# mixed. Normalising here deletes the whole class of problem. Write whatever
# your editor produces; git stores LF.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs. binary; `eol=lf` pins the working-tree
# ending too, so behaviour does not depend on a per-machine git config.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats git must never translate. `text=auto` already detects these
# correctly, but naming them means a misconfigured tool cannot corrupt an asset
# by guessing wrong.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.svgz  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.wav   binary
*.webm  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
